// v0.3-M3e compile-error gallery
//
// M3e ships full cross-module frame serialization: the compiler's codegen-side
// frame_layouts_query resolves each imported suspending callee's LLVM-accurate
// FrameLayout so the importer's state machine can embed the sub-frame at the
// right offset. All analyzable cross-module suspending call shapes now compile
// and run correctly.
//
// The former universal-reject diagnostic ("Calling `{name}` across a module
// boundary isn't supported yet …") is REMOVED — the feature it pointed at has
// shipped. Cross-module suspending calls no longer trigger it.
//
// REJECTS THAT REMAIN after M3e:
// These are the genuinely-unanalyzable cases — the compiler CANNOT determine
// the callee's suspension status statically, so a cross-module sub-frame embed
// is impossible. They reject via the existing may-block unresolvable-edge path
// (NOT via the deleted universal-reject guard).
//
// (a) Dynamic dispatch through a `dynamic Contract` vtable
//     The callee is resolved at runtime — the compiler cannot read its
//     FrameLayout at compile time. Rejects with a WHAT/WHAT-INSTEAD/WHY
//     diagnostic naming the vtable and the vtable call site.
//     Single-file — demonstrable directly below.
//
// (b) FFI cross-module suspending calls
//     A suspending function called through Yinz's foreign-function interface
//     (when FFI ships) cannot be analyzed statically — the callee's FrameLayout
//     lives outside the Yinz compiler's view. FFI is not yet reachable in
//     v0.3-M3e (no FFI surface exists). When FFI ships, this class rejects
//     via the same unresolvable-edge path with a tailored diagnostic.
//
// All remaining rejects emit WHAT/WHAT-INSTEAD/WHY diagnostics (exit 1) and
// do NOT produce SIGILL (exit 132). The compiler is safe — it refuses to emit
// a mis-sized frame rather than crash at runtime.

// ── (a) DynamicDispatch: cross-module suspension through a vtable ─────────────
//
// WHY: DynamicDispatch — calling a method on a `dynamic Contract` value from
// inside a suspending function is rejected. The callee is dispatched at runtime
// through a vtable; the compiler cannot determine at compile time whether the
// concrete callee suspends, so it cannot compute a FrameLayout for sub-frame
// embedding. The unresolvable-edge reject fires with a WHAT/WHAT-INSTEAD/WHY
// diagnostic naming the vtable type and the call site.
//
// The diagnostic emitted (WHAT / WHAT-INSTEAD / WHY):
//
//   WHAT: Can't determine whether `doWork` suspends — it's a dynamic-dispatch
//         call through a `dynamic Worker` vtable.
//
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Make the boundary explicit: use a concrete type instead of
//                 `dynamic Worker`, or restructure so the call is statically
//                 resolvable from inside a suspending function.
//
//   WHY: Dynamic dispatch resolves the callee at runtime — the compiler can't
//        determine its suspension status at compile time. v0.3-M2 requires
//        static resolution for calls from suspending functions; dynamic dispatch
//        support for suspending callees ships in a future version.
//
// To see this diagnostic, run the single-file fixture that exercises it:
//   ynz build crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m2_cant_infer_dynamic_dispatch.ynz
//
// The fixture shape (single-file, because cross-module is not needed to trigger it):
//
//   shape Worker {
//     doWork(share self) -> nothing
//   }
//   function runWithDynamicWorker(w: dynamic Worker) -> nothing {
//     sleep(10)
//     w.doWork()   ← DynamicDispatch reject fires here
//   }
//
// Multi-module dynamic-dispatch rejects behave identically: if an imported module
// exposes a function that takes `dynamic Contract` and calls a vtable method while
// suspending, the same reject fires at the module-boundary call site. The reject
// is not cross-module-specific — it fires wherever a vtable call appears inside a
// suspending context, regardless of which module defines the function.

// ── (b) FFI cross-module suspending calls (not yet reachable) ────────────────
//
// WHY: FFI — a Yinz function that calls a foreign (C/C++/Rust) function
// declared via the FFI interface cannot be statically analyzed for suspension.
// The foreign callee's FrameLayout is outside the Yinz compiler's view.
// When FFI ships in a future version, this class will reject via the
// unresolvable-edge path with a tailored diagnostic. Today (v0.3-M3e) there
// is no FFI surface in Yinz, so this class is not reachable.
//
// When FFI does ship, the expected diagnostic pattern:
//
//   WHAT: Can't determine whether `<foreign_fn>` suspends — it's declared
//         via FFI and its suspension status is unknown to the compiler.
//
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Wrap the FFI call in a non-suspending helper, or ensure
//                 `<foreign_fn>` is called only from a non-suspending context.
//
//   WHY: Foreign functions have no Yinz FrameLayout. A suspending caller cannot
//        embed a sub-frame for a callee whose memory layout is unknown.
